── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #2575  overall: potential_risk
Spec: mseccfgh must be gated on mseccfg presence, not just XLEN=32; software checking only XLEN will access a nonexistent CSR and leak data or trap
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:429-430  (header macro definitions)
      CSR_MSECCFG (0x747) and CSR_MSECCFGH (0x757) are defined unconditionally with no comment documenting the existence dependency: mseccfgh exists only when (XLEN=32 AND mseccfg is implemented). Grep confirms zero callers in the tree — these are dead-code definitions that invite future misuse. Any future developer who writes csr_write(CSR_MSECCFGH, val) without first checking that mseccfg is implemented will trigger an unimplemented-CSR trap or read garbage on XLEN=32 systems lacking mseccfg, leaking internal state. The spec requires that software verify the base CSR (mseccfg) is implemented before touching its high-half alias.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:245-249  (header macro definitions)
      MSECCFG_PMM* bit macros are aliased from ENVCFG_PMM* bits and are completely orphaned — zero callers outside csr.h. Their presence alongside the bare CSR_MSECCFG/CSR_MSECCFGH definitions creates a false impression that mseccfg is a universally-present, directly-accessible CSR. This misleads future developers into writing direct mseccfg/mseccfgh accessors using these bit definitions, bypassing the safe SBI_FWFT delegation path. The bit definitions themselves are not buggy, but they constitute an incomplete API surface that lacks the corresponding existence-check guard required by the spec.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:174-186  .Lsecondary_start_common / smp_callin
      This code uses the safe indirect delegation path: it invokes SBI_EXT_FWFT with SBI_FWFT_SHADOW_STACK to request M-mode firmware program mseccfg.SSE, rather than touching CSR_MSECCFG or CSR_MSECCFGH directly. The M-mode SBI firmware is responsible for knowing whether mseccfg is implemented and handling the CSR access safely. The code correctly checks the SBI return value (beqz a0) and falls back to disabling user CFI if the SBI call fails. No direct CSR access to mseccfg or mseccfgh occurs.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:347-359  (boot CPU init path)
      Identical safe SBI_FWFT_SHADOW_STACK delegation pattern repeated for the boot CPU path. Same analysis applies: no direct CSR_MSECCFG/CSR_MSECCFGH access, SBI return value is checked, and a fallback disables user CFI on failure. Compliant with the spec because the M-mode SBI layer handles the existence-check internally.

── opensbi ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #2575  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: mseccfgh must be gated on mseccfg presence, not just XLEN=32; software checking only XLEN will access a nonexistent CSR and leak data or trap
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:704-706  hart_detect_features
      csr_read(CSR_MSECCFG) is executed with only DT-based guard sbi_hart_has_extension(SMEPMP). The SMEPMP extension bit is set from parsing 'riscv,isa-extensions' or 'riscv,isa' in the FDT (fdt_parse_isa_extensions_one_hart does a string match on 'smepmp'). There is no hardware CSR-existence probe via csr_read_allowed() for CSR_MSECCFG anywhere in hart_detect_features, unlike every other extension CSR (SCOUNTOVF, MTOPI, STIMECMP, MSTATEEN0, etc.) which ARE verified with __check_ext_csr(). If the DT claims Smepmp but mseccfg is unimplemented on the silicon, this csr_read() causes an illegal-instruction trap in M-mode with no trap handler, which is fatal. CSR_MSECCFG is absent from enum sbi_hart_csrs so the sbi_hart_has_csr() guard cannot even be used.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:139-139  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      csr_set(CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_RLB) writes mseccfg with only DT-based SMEPMP guard at the init call site (sbi_hart_pmp_init:335). No hardware probe confirms CSR_MSECCFG actually exists. On hardware where DT lists 'smepmp' but the CSR is unimplemented, this CSR write either traps (no M-mode write handler → fatal) or silently no-ops, leaving RLB unset. If RLB is unset, subsequent PMP programming (lines 142-180) may be enforced rather than bypassed, silently breaking PMP configuration. This is a CSR-write trap in M-mode with the same fatal consequence as the read site.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:185-185  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      csr_set(CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_MML) — same CSR, same function, same missing hardware-probe guard as line 139. Practically, if line 139 already trapped fatally this line is unreachable; if line 139 succeeded then mseccfg exists and this is safe. Flagged as potential_risk rather than bug_confirmed because it is a dependent site: it only becomes dangerous independently if some future code path reaches it without passing through line 139 first.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:177-179  mstatus_init
      csr_set/clear(CSR_MSECCFG, SSEED/USEED) guarded by SBI_HART_EXT_ZKR from DT parsing. Zkr exercises USEED/SSEED bits defined in mseccfg (Smepmp). If DT claims 'zkr' but the silicon only implements Zkr entropy source without Smepmp/mseccfg, these CSR writes trap. In practice Zkr-surfaced implementations nearly always bundle mseccfg, but the invariant 'Zkr implies mseccfg exists' is NOT enforced in code. Marked potential_risk because the practical likelihood is lower than the Smepmp sites.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:727-740  (header definitions)
      This header defines CSR_MSECCFG (0x747), CSR_MSECCFGH (0x757), and MSECCFG bitfield macros (MML, MMWP, RLB, USEED, SSEED). These are reference definitions and do not themselves access any CSR. CSR_MSECCFGH is defined but unreferenced anywhere in the codebase — a minor observation but not a bug. The actual risk is at the code sites that use these macros without hardware CSR-existence checks.

